(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-eye-ear-anatomy-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Eye and Ear Anatomy — Anatomy Exam 2 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Into what three regions is the ear subdivided?","choices":["Anterior, middle, and posterior ear","External ear, middle ear, and inner ear","Just external and inner ear, omitting the middle","Utricle, saccule, and pinna only"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ear is subdivided into three regions: the external ear, middle ear, and inner ear. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What structures make up the external ear?","choices":["Auricle, external acoustic meatus, tympanic membrane, and ceruminous glands","Auditory tube (pharyngotympanic tube) and tympanic cavity only","Malleus, incus, and stapes, suspended within the auricle's cartilage","Cochlea and semicircular canals, embedded within the auricle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The external ear consists of the auricle, external acoustic meatus, tympanic membrane, and ceruminous glands. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What structures make up the middle ear?","choices":["Tympanic cavity, auditory ossicles (malleus, incus, stapes), and auditory tube (pharyngotympanic tube)","Auricle and external acoustic meatus, along with the ceruminous glands","Cochlea, vestibule, and semicircular canals, surrounding the oval window","Only the tympanic membrane, suspended freely within the tympanic cavity"],"answer":0,"exp":"The middle ear consists of the tympanic cavity, the auditory ossicles (malleus, incus, and stapes), and the auditory tube (pharyngotympanic tube). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What structures make up the inner ear?","choices":["Tympanic cavity and auditory ossicles only, without any membranous or fluid-filled structures","Only the cochlea, with no other structures, since the vestibule is classified separately","Auricle, external acoustic meatus, and tympanic membrane, surrounding the oval window","Receptors, membranous labyrinth (within the bony labyrinth), vestibule, semicircular canals, cochlea, utricle, and saccule"],"answer":3,"exp":"The inner ear consists of receptors, the membranous labyrinth (within the bony labyrinth), the vestibule, semicircular canals, cochlea, utricle, and saccule. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What are the three auditory ossicles, and where does the base of the stapes attach?","choices":["Only two ossicles: malleus and stapes, with the stapes base attaching at the round window","Malleus, incus, and stapes; the base of the stapes attaches at the oval window","Stapes, cochlea, and vestibule, with the stapes base attaching at the round window","Malleus, incus, and vestibule; the base attaches at the tympanic membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"The three auditory ossicles are the malleus, incus, and stapes; the base of the stapes attaches at the oval window. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What does the Eustachian (auditory) tube do, and why is its horizontal positioning in children clinically relevant?","choices":["Equalizes pressure in the middle ear and allows fluid drainage; in children, its horizontal position results in stasis of fluid","Only produces earwax, unrelated to pressure regulation; its horizontal position in children affects hearing sensitivity","Connects the cochlea to the vestibule, allowing endolymph exchange; its position in children affects balance, not pressure","Has no clinical relevance regardless of positioning, since pressure equalizes passively through the tympanic membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"The Eustachian tube equalizes pressure in the middle ear and allows fluid to drain from the middle ear; it is positioned horizontally in children, which results in stasis of fluid (a clinically relevant point, since fluid doesn't drain as efficiently). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the relationship between the bony labyrinth and membranous labyrinth, and what fluids fill each space?","choices":["Both labyrinths are filled with the identical fluid, since perilymph and endolymph have nearly indistinguishable compositions","The bony labyrinth lies within the membranous labyrinth; endolymph surrounds it, and perilymph fills the space inside","The membranous labyrinth lies within the bony labyrinth; perilymph fills the space around the membranous labyrinth, and endolymph fills within the membranous labyrinth","Neither labyrinth contains any fluid; both spaces are instead filled with a gelatinous connective tissue matrix"],"answer":2,"exp":"The membranous labyrinth lies within the bony labyrinth; perilymph fills the space between the bony and membranous labyrinth, while endolymph fills within the membranous labyrinth itself. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the vestibular complex, and what does it detect?","choices":["Part of the middle ear that amplifies sound via the ossicular chain before it reaches the cochlea","Part of the inner ear providing equilibrium sensations by detecting rotation, gravity, and acceleration","Part of the external ear that collects sound waves and funnels them toward the tympanic membrane","A structure detecting only color vision, located within the retina rather than the ear"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vestibular complex is the part of the inner ear that provides equilibrium sensations by detecting rotation, gravity, and acceleration, consisting of the semicircular canals, utricle, saccule, and crista ampularis. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What structures are found within the ampulla of each semicircular canal, and what do hair cells contain?","choices":["A cristae with hair cells; each hair cell contains a kinocilium and stereocilia embedded in the cupula","Statoconia crystals only, with no hair cells, suspended freely within the endolymph of the ampulla","The organ of Corti, resting on the basilar membrane within the ampulla of each canal","Only fluid, with no cellular structures, allowing free endolymph movement through the ampulla"],"answer":0,"exp":"Within each ampulla is a cristae with hair cells; each hair cell contains a kinocilium and stereocilia, which are embedded in gelatinous material called the cupula. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"How does head movement translate into a sensory signal within the semicircular canals?","choices":["Movement of the body has no effect on the endolymph, since the cupula responds only to gravitational pull on otoliths","Movement of the body causes movement of endolymph in the canal, which moves the cupula and hair cells, detected by the brain","The cupula moves independently of endolymph, driven instead by pressure changes transmitted from the middle ear","Perilymph (not endolymph) moves within the semicircular canals, bending the cupula and stimulating hair cells directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"The movement of the body causes movement of fluid (endolymph) in the semicircular canal, which in turn causes movement of the cupula and hair cells, which the brain detects. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What are maculae, and what is an otolith?","choices":["Maculae are found only in the cochlea, arranged along the organ of Corti; otoliths are a specialized nerve fiber in the vestibule","Neither structure is related to equilibrium; maculae instead detect sound frequency, and otoliths regulate endolymph production","Maculae are clusters of photoreceptor cells found in the retina; otoliths are found in the eye, embedded in the vitreous humor","Maculae are clusters of hair cells in the utricle/saccule; an otolith is the gelatinous material plus statoconia (calcium carbonate crystals) covering them"],"answer":3,"exp":"Maculae are clusters of hair cells in the utricle and saccule; an otolith is the gelatinous material and statoconia (calcium carbonate crystals) collectively that embed these hair cells. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What happens to the otoliths when moving upward (elevator movement) versus downward?","choices":["Otoliths only respond to side-to-side tilting, never vertical movement, since gravity acts uniformly on elevator direction","Moving upward: otoliths lift off the macular surface; moving downward: otoliths press down onto the macular surface","Otoliths remain completely stationary regardless of vertical movement, since their mass is too great to shift with brief accelerations","Moving upward: otoliths press down on the macular surface; moving downward: otoliths lift off the macular surface"],"answer":3,"exp":"When moving upward, the otoliths press down on the macular surface; when moving downward, the otoliths lift off the macular surface. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which semicircular duct is associated with the \"yes\" head-nodding plane, and which with the \"no\" head-shaking plane?","choices":["All three ducts detect identical head movements","Anterior semicircular duct: \"yes\"; lateral semicircular duct: \"no\"","Lateral semicircular duct: \"yes\"; posterior semicircular duct: \"no\"","Posterior semicircular duct: \"yes\"; anterior semicircular duct: \"no\""],"answer":1,"exp":"The anterior semicircular duct is associated with the \"yes\" (nodding) plane of head movement, the lateral semicircular duct with the \"no\" (shaking) plane, and the posterior semicircular duct with \"tilting the head.\" — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Why does spinning in a circle and then stopping abruptly cause dizziness, per the material?","choices":["Dizziness occurs only in the cochlea, unrelated to the vestibular system, resulting from residual sound-induced hair cell fatigue","Perilymph (not endolymph) is responsible for this dizziness effect, continuing to swirl within the bony labyrinth after stopping","The endolymph immediately stops moving the instant the body stops, causing a brief pressure spike the brain misreads as motion","The endolymph continues moving back and forth after stopping, causing the hair cells to bend back and forth, resulting in confusing signals"],"answer":3,"exp":"When you move in a circle and then stop abruptly, the endolymph continues moving back and forth, causing the hair cells to bend back and forth, resulting in confusing signals and thus dizziness. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the modiolus, and what does it contain?","choices":["A structure found in the vestibule, unrelated to the cochlea, that anchors the utricle and saccule to the temporal bone","A membrane separating the scala vestibuli and scala tympani, formed from a thin layer of connective tissue","The outermost layer of the cochlea; contains no neural structures, serving purely as a protective bony shell","The central area around which the cochlea's spirals coil; contains sensory neurons associated with CN VIII"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cochlea consists of \"snail-shaped\" spirals that coil around a central area called the modiolus; within the modiolus are sensory neurons associated with CN VIII. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What are the three layers (ducts) of each cochlear spiral, and what fluid does each contain?","choices":["Only two layers exist: scala vestibuli and scala tympani, both containing endolymph throughout","All three layers contain identical fluid, allowing free exchange of ions between each duct","Vestibular duct (endolymph), tympanic duct (endolymph), and cochlear duct (perilymph)","Scala vestibuli (perilymph), scala tympani (perilymph), and scala media (endolymph, located between the other two)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Each cochlear spiral consists of three layers: the scala vestibuli (vestibular duct, containing perilymph), the scala tympani (tympanic duct, containing perilymph), and the scala media (cochlear duct, containing endolymph), located between the scala vestibuli and scala tympani. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Where are the scala vestibuli and scala tympani connected, and where do sense organs rest?","choices":["Sense organs rest directly on the modiolus, not the basilar membrane, and the ducts connect at the round window","Not connected at any point, since the scala vestibuli and scala tympani remain entirely separate fluid systems","Connected at the apical end of the cochlea; sense organs rest on the basilar membrane within the scala media","Connected only at the cochlea's base; sense organs instead rest within the scala tympani"],"answer":2,"exp":"The scala vestibuli and scala tympani are connected at the apical end of the cochlea; sense organs rest on the basilar membrane within the scala media. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Where does the Organ of Corti (spiral organ) rest, and what membrane are its hair cells in contact with?","choices":["Rests on the basilar membrane between the scala media and scala tympani; hair cells are in contact with the overlying tectorial membrane","Rests on the tectorial membrane; hair cells contact the basilar membrane on the opposite side","Rests within the vestibule, unrelated to the cochlea, anchored to the utricle's macula","Rests on the oval window directly, with hair cells contacting the stapes footplate"],"answer":0,"exp":"The Organ of Corti (spiral organ) rests on the basilar membrane between the scala media and the scala tympani; its hair cells are in contact with the overlying tectorial membrane, which is attached to the lining of the scala media. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What ultimately stimulates the Organ of Corti, causing hearing?","choices":["Sound waves cause distortion of the tectorial membrane, stimulating the Organ of Corti","Changes in otolith position, transmitted through the vestibule to the hair cells","Direct contact of sound waves with the modiolus, bypassing the tectorial membrane entirely","Rotation of the head, unrelated to sound, sensed by the semicircular canal ducts"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sound waves ultimately cause a distortion of the tectorial membrane, thus stimulating the Organ of Corti. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is the initial sequence of events in the auditory pathway, from sound waves entering the ear to perilymph movement?","choices":["Sound waves directly stimulate the Organ of Corti without any intermediate structures, bypassing the tympanic membrane","Perilymph moves first, causing the tympanic membrane to vibrate afterward, which then sets the ossicular chain in motion","Sound waves enter the external acoustic meatus → tympanic membrane vibrates → ossicles vibrate → stapes vibrates against the oval window → perilymph begins to move","The auditory tube directly transmits sound waves to the cochlea, bypassing the tympanic membrane and ossicular chain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sound waves enter the external acoustic meatus, the tympanic membrane vibrates, this causes vibration of the ossicles, the stapes vibrates against the oval window of the scala vestibuli, and perilymph begins to move. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"After perilymph begins to move, what happens to the scala media, the hair cells of the Organ of Corti, and where is the signal ultimately sent?","choices":["Nerve signals are sent to the brain via CN VII, after pressure on the scala vestibuli directly bends the hair cells","Pressure is put on the scala vestibuli only, with no effect on hair cells, since the scala media stays isolated from perilymph","Pressure is put on the scala media, distorting the hair cells of the Organ of Corti, depolarizing neurons, and sending nerve signals to the brain via CN VIII","The endolymph in the semicircular canals is affected instead, transmitting pressure changes via CN VIII to the vestibular nucleus"],"answer":2,"exp":"As the perilymph moves, pressure is put on the scala media, this pressure distorts the hair cells of the Organ of Corti, this distortion depolarizes the neurons, and nerve signals are sent to the brain via CN VIII (the vestibulocochlear nerve). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Within the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) pathway, where do cochlear nerve fibers first synapse after leaving the cochlea?","choices":["The vestibular nucleus","The auditory cortex directly","The superior olivary nucleus directly, bypassing the cochlear nuclei","The cochlear nuclei"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cochlear branch fibers of CN VIII first synapse in the cochlear nuclei, before continuing on to structures like the superior olivary nucleus, inferior colliculus, and medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus, ultimately reaching the auditory cortex. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Where does the auditory pathway ultimately terminate, and in which lobe is this cortex located?","choices":["The primary somatosensory cortex, located in the parietal lobe","The primary motor cortex, located in the frontal lobe","The visual cortex, located in the occipital lobe","The auditory cortex, located in the temporal lobe"],"answer":3,"exp":"The auditory pathway ultimately terminates at the auditory cortex, located in the temporal lobe. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"The chorda tympani nerve, found within the middle ear cavity, is a branch of which cranial nerve?","choices":["CN VII (facial nerve)","CN VIII (vestibulocochlear nerve)","CN IX (glossopharyngeal nerve)","CN V (trigeminal nerve)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The chorda tympani nerve, seen within the structures of the middle ear cavity, is a branch of CN VII (the facial nerve). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Where do vestibular branch fibers of CN VIII first synapse, and what two general destinations does the pathway continue to?","choices":["Synapse directly in the cerebral cortex, bypassing all brainstem nuclei, before projecting onward to the cerebellum","Synapse at the red nucleus only, with no further connections, terminating before reaching higher processing centers","Synapse at the vestibular nucleus, continuing via vestibulospinal tracts (to the spinal cord) and to the cerebellum, as well as relay to the cerebral cortex via the superior colliculus","Synapse at the cochlear nuclei, identical to the cochlear branch pathway, before diverging toward the auditory cortex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Vestibular branch fibers of CN VIII synapse at the vestibular nucleus, with the pathway continuing via vestibulospinal tracts (to the spinal cord) and to the cerebellum, as well as relaying to the cerebral cortex via the superior colliculus; the vestibular nucleus also connects with CN III, IV, VI, and XI nuclei. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve nuclei are shown connecting with the vestibular nucleus, relevant to coordinating eye and head movement with balance input?","choices":["CN I, II, V, and VII only","CN III, IV, VI, and XI","CN IX, X, and XII only","CN VIII only, with no other cranial nerve connections"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vestibular nucleus is shown connecting with the nuclei of CN III, IV, VI, and XI, relevant to coordinating eye movements (via the oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens nerves) and head/neck movements (via the accessory nerve) with vestibular balance input. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What does the vestibular membrane separate within the cochlear duct system?","choices":["Separates the organ of Corti from the tectorial membrane, preventing contact between hair cell stereocilia and the membrane","Is unrelated to any duct separation within the cochlea, functioning instead solely as support for the modiolus","Separates the tympanic duct from the vestibular duct directly, without the scala media positioned between them","Separates the vestibular duct (scala vestibuli) from the cochlear duct (scala media)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vestibular membrane separates the vestibular duct (scala vestibuli) from the cochlear duct (scala media) within the cochlear spiral. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What are the inner hair cell and outer hair cell, within the Organ of Corti?","choices":["Neither cell type is related to hearing; both instead function as supporting cells anchoring the basilar membrane","Only one type of hair cell exists in the Organ of Corti, arranged in a single row along its entire length","Both are types of hair cells within the Organ of Corti, in contact with the tectorial membrane above the basilar membrane","The inner hair cell is found in the vestibule; the outer hair cell is found in the cochlea's scala vestibuli"],"answer":2,"exp":"The inner hair cell and outer hair cell are both types of hair cells within the Organ of Corti, positioned on the basilar membrane and in contact with the overlying tectorial membrane, contributing to sound transduction. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What type of cartilage is found within the auricle of the external ear?","choices":["Fibrocartilage","No cartilage; the auricle is composed entirely of bone","Hyaline cartilage","Elastic cartilage"],"answer":3,"exp":"The auricle of the external ear contains elastic cartilage. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the relationship between the oval window and round window, in terms of the structures that interact with each?","choices":["The round window is where the stapes attaches, not the oval window, which instead serves only as a passive pressure-release membrane at the end of the tympanic duct","The stapes base sits at the oval window (transmitting vibration into the scala vestibuli); the round window is a separate membrane-covered opening allowing pressure release at the end of the tympanic duct","Both windows are attached directly to the stapes, transmitting vibration equally into the scala vestibuli and scala tympani","Neither window is related to the auditory ossicles or cochlear ducts, functioning only as passive openings in the temporal bone"],"answer":1,"exp":"The base of the stapes sits at the oval window, transmitting vibrations into the scala vestibuli (vestibular duct), while the round window is a separate, membrane-covered opening that allows pressure release at the end of the tympanic duct pathway. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 41"}]};
